3) Key implementation components & how they solve your problems
A. Replace static SPNs with dynamic SPNs (Azure secrets engine)
Vault Azure secrets engine can create Azure service principals with roles and TTLs on demand.

Instead of storing long-lived secret clients, apps request a credential, use it, and the credential expires automatically (rotation becomes unnecessary).

Vault holds an Azure management credential (ideally a limited SPN with just Application.ReadWrite.All / role-assignment permission for the subscription/resource scope required to create SPNs) or use a federated identity flow if available.

Example role (Vault CLI):

vault secrets enable azure

vault write azure/config \
    tenant_id="00000000-1111-2222-3333-444444444444" \
    client_id="VAULT_MGMT_SP_CLIENT_ID" \
    client_secret="VAULT_MGMT_SP_SECRET" \
    subscription_id="your-sub"

vault write azure/roles/my-aks-role \
    ttl="1h" \
    azure_roles='[{"role_name":"Contributor","scope":"/subscriptions/your-sub/resourceGroups/my-rg"}]'
When an app requests credentials, Vault creates an SPN, assigns role(s), returns client_id/client_secret and sets TTL.

If you need persistent long-lived SPNs for legacy reasons: still create them via Vault but set long TTL and put rotation workflows (Vault can rotate via re-issuing).

B. AKS workloads — Kubernetes auth + Vault CSI / Agent Injector
Enable Kubernetes auth in Vault so pods authenticate with a Kubernetes service account.

Use Vault CSI provider (recommended) to mount secrets as files into pods (supports renewals) or Vault Agent Injector to inject env vars.

Secrets can be dynamic (SPNs) or static secrets read from Vault; CSI handles refresh/renewal when TTLs change.

Flow:

Pod uses service account that Vault maps to a Vault policy.

Pod requests azure/creds/<role> through CSI; Vault returns short TTL SPN material and mounts it.

App uses credentials (no restart required, CSI can handle file refresh).

C. Non-AKS apps — AppRole / Azure AD Auth
For non-K8s apps (VMs, on-prem), prefer:

Azure AD Auth in Vault if those machines have Managed Identity (MSI) — they authenticate to Vault via Azure tokens.

AppRole where you provision role_id/secret_id (secret_id can be delivered using response wrapping or one-time channels).

Use short-lived tokens and rotate secret IDs periodically.

D. Certificates automation — PKI engine + ACME connector (if public)
Use Vault PKI to create internal CA and issue certs for internal domains automatically (TTL-based renewal).

For public/trusted certificates:

Either integrate Vault with an external CA via intermediate signing or use ACME (Let's Encrypt) automation pipeline where Vault acts as a client to ACME to fetch certs and store them.

Or have Vault as a private CA and use your infrastructure to trust that CA.

Provide a REST/CLI/API for teams to request certs; store cert and key in Vault; deliver to AKS via CSI or to infra via AppRole.

E. One-time secret sharing
Use Response Wrapping (wrapped tokens): Vault returns a wrapping token that can be used exactly once to unwrap and read the secret in the cubbyhole.

Use short ttl wrapped tokens for human or machine one-time delivery (e.g., via Slack/Email link).

Or use cubbyhole for app-to-app one-time handoff.

