6) Rotation & lifecycle management patterns
Prefer dynamic credentials: TTL-driven, no manual rotation needed.

For static secrets that must exist: automate rotation via Vault periodic jobs (use Vault API to re-issue and update apps).

For ACK/legacy apps where env var must change: Use a sidecar that watches Vault and updates env or restart on secret change (better: move to CSI file mount).

7) Security hardening & governance
Least privilege: Vault policies grant minimal paths. Azure credential used by Vault has minimal rights (only create SPNs/assign roles in required scope).

Namespaces: use Vault namespaces to segregate teams/environments.

Audit logging: Enable Vault audit devices (file, syslog, send to Azure Monitor/Log Analytics).

TLS everywhere: Vault API must be TLS-terminated via Ingress/Service with proper certs (use certs from PKI).

Sealed/Unsealing: Use auto-unseal via Azure Key Vault (or HSM) to avoid manual unseal. If you self-host Vault on AKS, configure auto-unseal via Azure Key Vault.

Storage backend: use Vault Integrated Storage (raft) for HA in-cluster, or Consul; ensure storage encryption at rest (Azure managed disks).

Secrets exposure minimization: Use response wrapping for sharing; avoid writing secrets to logs.

Periodic key rotation & revocation: maintain CRON jobs that check for expired creds and revoke orphaned credentials.

